; AlifeTactics Reaction tuning. Paired with gamedata/scripts/at_reaction.script. All keys have script-side fallback defaults, so a missing key or file won't crash. The MCM Reaction
; sliders read their defaults from this file; a slider the player moved overrides that key, and "reset to defaults" returns to these values.
;
; Difficulty paradigm (2026-08-05): base = the flat vanilla value on every curve (nothing sits below the base game, so low ranks never compound into a pushover), the per-rank percent
; climbs toward a decent legend kept well short of the game's own hardcore-aim preset (harder, not aimbot). Vanilla rank scaling is non-functional - ai_stalker.cpp:761 clamps Rank() to
; [0,100] but Anomaly ranks run to the thousands, so m_fRankDisperison collapses to a constant and immunities/visibility ship 1.0/1.0 (game_relations.ltx); every curve here is a script-side
; rank axis over a flat baseline, not a retune of a working one.
;
;   value(tier) = base * (1 + rank_pct/100 * tier_index),  tier_index = 0 (novice) .. 7 (legend)
;
; Each pair is least-squares fitted to the retired 8-value tables (2026-10-18, t232); the aim and track legends land softer
; than the old hand-bent endpoints (aim 1.31 vs 1.50, track 0.37 vs 0.40) - the ruled field check covers them.
; src (vanilla aim endpoints): sight_manager.cpp:75-76 g_ai_aim_min_speed 0.24, g_ai_aim_min_angle PI/16 = 0.196; the hardcore-aim option (ui_options.script) sets min_speed 2.5 / min_angle 17,
; our legends stay far below that.
;
; aim is rad/s, the sight fine-tracking speed cap (select_speed, sight_manager.cpp:80-101): how fast the barrel moves inside the min_angle lock band. Base 0.24 = vanilla.
; Paired with track below; speed alone, at the vanilla 0.196 lock band, barely touches hit% (forced A/B, 10x spread, no first-shot or strafing-hit% change), it matters once track widens the band.

[at_reaction]
; turn_max/turn_falloff_m: the hit-victim turn (set_hit_redirect, PR #636); the last attacker within falloff metres gets up to turn_max subtracted from his selection cost, 900/60
; probe-proven to flip selection at ~10m. An always-on targeting fix, bound per NPC at spawn beside the curves below.
turn_max       = 900
turn_falloff_m = 60

aim_base     = 0.24
aim_rank_pct = 63

; track is rad/s, the min_angle band (ai_aim_min_angle, sight_manager.cpp:76): the gap below which the barrel tracks at min_speed with no deceleration. Vanilla 0.196 is half the fire cone,
; so the barrel eases off at the cone edge and a strafer can juke it; raising it toward the fire cone (0.3927) and past keeps the barrel locked across the whole firing window, the real
; hard-to-dodge dial (Tracking Lock). Base = vanilla 0.196; the climb stays under max_angle 0.785 so the natural fast swing-in survives (VERIFY(max>min)).
track_base     = 0.196
track_rank_pct = 12

; lead_* is the per-rank lead error (t156): the rank's multiplier on the physically-correct lead. The engine leads a moving target by aim_point += target_velocity * predict_time
; (sight_action.cpp:383-384); the correct predict_time is the bullet's flight time = range / bullet_speed (the weapon's bullet_speed times the round's k_bullet_speed, ShootingObject.cpp:168
; and Level_Bullet_Manager.cpp:66, so subsonic/AP ammo scales the lead). at_reaction recomputes predict_time per firing NPC on the fire seam (throttled 1s) as clamp(range / bullet_speed,
; 0, 0.5) * lead_<tier>, so the lead adapts to range and round speed on its own; lead_* is the only rank lever. Legend 1.00 leads true and hits movers, novice 2.00 over-leads, linear between.
; Skill is judging the lead right, and the error compounds with the wide low-rank dispersion cone. Bounded and range-scaled, unlike the retired fixed 0.40->0.28 table that over-led every rank close in.
lead_base     = 2.00
lead_rank_pct = -7.4

; vision_* is the per-rank reaction/detection-speed factor: it multiplies get_visible_value accumulation (visual_memory_manager.cpp:365-379) after whichever detection stack the install runs
; (engine formula or a Lua get_visible_value functor), so it is a relative rank spread over the installed baseline. It is reaction, how fast a stalker turns a glimpse into a confirmed threat,
; not eyesight: detection timing only, never aim or accuracy. Every install is rank-blind at detection (vanilla: one [stalker_vision_*] set for all stalkers, m_stalker.ltx:76-99); this adds
; the rank axis over that baseline, novice 1.00 = vanilla (retuned 2026-08-05 so no rank detects slower than the base game) to legend 1.20 (+20%), the prior spread shifted up to sit at or above vanilla.
vision_base     = 1.00
vision_rank_pct = 2.9

; vrange_* is the per-rank detection-distance factor, the twin of vision_* on the other vision axis: it multiplies object_visible_distance (visual_memory_manager.cpp:303-318), setting how
; far out a stalker begins to notice a target where vision_ sets how fast once in sight. at_reaction registers it as the rank slice of at_effects_resolver's VISION_RANGE effect, combined
; highest-wins with a carried optic's factor; the resolver writes it at net_spawn via set_view_distance_factor. A tight band centred on vanilla, novice 1.00 to legend 1.15, the same shape as
; vision speed. Dormant until the n038 view-distance bind exists on the exe; the setter no-ops below it, so the curve is inert but live the moment the bind arrives.
vrange_base     = 1.00
vrange_rank_pct = 2.1

; qsize_* / qinterval_* are the two fire-queue scales (t154), fed to npc:set_fire_queue_scale (#603) at spawn: qsize_ multiplies the combat planner's burst size, qinterval_ the inter-burst
; pause (select_queue_params, stalker_combat_action_base.cpp:246-254). Higher rank = crisper short bursts (qsize < 1) at a tighter cadence (qinterval < 1). Non-degradation invariant:
; qsize >= qinterval at every tier. rounds/min is proportional to size/(interval + size*dt), so qsize >= qinterval keeps rounds/min >= vanilla; a shorter burst drops only the dispersed tail
; rounds (past base_dispersioned_bullets_count, WeaponMagazined.cpp:797-800), so per-shot% rises. Volume >= vanilla plus per-shot% >= vanilla gives hits/min >= vanilla
; (doc/library/modding/npc-combat-effectiveness.md, the queue section). Novice 1.00/1.00 = exact vanilla. Near-flat spans on purpose: the per-rank axis stays barely visible out of the box,
; the MCM sliders still allow a wide spread.
qsize_base         = 1.00
qsize_rank_pct     = -0.6
qinterval_base     = 1.00
qinterval_rank_pct = -0.7
